{
  "id": "Compound::DB01274 - Gene::153",
  "verbalization": "Arformoterol, a well-known adrenergic beta-2 agonist, is consistently linked to the Adrb1 receptor through multiple pathways in the hetionet dataset. The evidence suggests that Arformoterol, as part of the broader class of adrenergic beta-2 agonists, binds to Adrb1, a key receptor involved in the regulation of heart rate and blood pressure. This binding is supported by the presence of Arformoterol in the Adrenergic Beta2-agonists category, which includes Pirbuterol and Salbutamol, all of which are known to interact with Adrb1. The shared ancestry of Arformoterol with these compounds, through the Beta-Adrenergic Agonist category, reinforces the biological relevance of this interaction.\n\nThe mechanism of action is further supported by the direct binding of these agonists to Adrb1, as evidenced by the lowest common ancestor relationships. The high scores in the final_score metric indicate a strong and consistent association across the dataset, suggesting a robust biological link. However, the evidence does not provide direct experimental validation, such as an IC50 value or in vitro binding studies, which would strengthen the mechanistic claim. The presence of multiple pathways converging on Adrb1 underscores the importance of this receptor in the context of adrenergic signaling and its potential role in therapeutic interventions.",
